  "worked_solution": "Newton\u2019s second law states that\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\nwhere $F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo compute the acceleration vector, we first differentiate $r$ to get the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82{e}^{t},\\,4{t}^{3}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate the velocity vector to get the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82{e}^{t},\\,12{t}^{2}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resultant force $F$ at time $t$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot \u27e82{e}^{t},\\,12{t}^{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e86{e}^{t},\\,36{t}^{2}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{s},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\,(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\u27e86{e}^{1/2},\\,36{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e86{e}^{1/2},\\,9\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of $F\\,(\\frac{1}{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|F\\,(\\frac{1}{2})| & =\\sqrt{(6{e}^{1/2})^{2}+(9)^{2}} \\\\  & \u224813\\,\\text{N},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nSo, the magnitude of the resultant force is $13\\,\\text{N}.$",
